060627 #edb/ref 1061 158250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060627 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "16ee2821-21ab-55ff-aa36-4acbd424ff94" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186060627 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186059735 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061001 #edb/ref 1064 "An expression is a **tautology** if it is always true, regardless of the truth values of inputs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the expression $A \\lor \\neg A,$ and fill out the corresponding truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,\\lnot A\\,$ | $\\,A \\lor \\neg A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n\nAccording to the table, $A \\lor \\neg A$ is always true. Therefore, it is a tautology.\n\nTautologies are represented by the symbol $\\textbf t,$ so we write\n\n$$A \\lor \\neg A = \\textbf t.$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061001 #edb/ref 1065 9141 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061001 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "55b05b11-cd0e-5642-b3e7-333b2b27cf0c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061001 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061002 #edb/ref 1064 "Tautologies have the following properties:\n\n- The disjunction of an expression and a tautology gives a tautology: $$A \\lor \\textbf{t} = \\textbf{t}$$\n- The conjunction of an expression and a tautology gives the initial expression: $$A \\land \\textbf{t} = A$$\n\nContradictions have the following properties:\n\n- The disjunction of an expression and a contradiction gives the initial expression: $$A \\lor \\textbf{c} = A$$\n- The conjunction of an expression and a contradiction gives a contradiction: $$A \\land \\textbf{c} = \\textbf{c}$$\n\nTo prove these properties, we need to fill out the corresponding truth tables. For example, the truth table for the first property is shown below.\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\textbf{t}$ | $A \\lor \\textbf{t}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm T}$ |\n\n According to the table, $A \\lor \\textbf{t}$ is always true. Therefore, it is a tautology.\n\nFinally, note the following:\n\n$$\\begin{aligned}\\text{t}∨\\text{t}=\\text{t}\\, & & \\text{t}∧\\text{t} & =\\text{t} \\\\ \\text{t}∨\\text{c}=\\text{t}\\, & & \\text{t}∧\\text{c} & =\\text{c} \\\\ \\text{c}∨\\text{t}=\\text{t}\\, & & \\text{c}∧\\text{t} & =\\text{c} \\\\ \\text{c}∨\\text{c}=\\text{c}\\, & & \\text{c}∧\\text{c} & =\\text{c}\\end{aligned}$$\n\nAgain, all of these properties can be proved using truth tables." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061002 #edb/ref 1065 9147 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061002 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "9e1edb93-5bcf-5372-87f2-0f30fe620e60" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061002 #edb/ref 1067 "Properties of Tautologies and Contradictions" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061009 #edb/ref 1064 "An expression is a **contradiction** if it is always false, regardless of the truth values of inputs.\n\nFor example, let's consider the expression $A \\land \\neg A,$ and fill out the corresponding truth table:\n\n| $\\,A\\,$ | $\\,\\lnot A\\,$ | $\\,A \\land \\neg A\\,$ |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| $\\textrm T$ | $\\textrm F$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n| $\\textrm F$ | $\\textrm T$ | $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\textrm F}$ |\n\nAccording to the table, $A \\land \\neg A$ is always false. Therefore, it is a contradiction.\n\nContradictions are represented by the symbol $\\textbf c,$ so we write\n\n$$A \\land \\neg A = \\textbf c.$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061009 #edb/ref 1065 9223 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061009 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "1f28d5b3-bd1c-56f5-92cd-e485de29b331" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186061009 #edb/ref 1067 "Contradictions" #edb/ref 17592186045417]}
